  "question": {
    "text": "Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> are two non-singular&nbsp;matrices of order <math><mn>3</mn></math> such that <math><mi>A</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>I</mi></math> and <math><msup><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>I</mi></math>, then <math><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></math> is equal to (where, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math>&nbsp;is the identity matrix of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>)",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
